First off, he's not a "new hire"; he pretty much designed the VM system
for Digital UNIX, so we feel *very* fortunate he's working with us now.

We're still trying to digest the last round of modifications in the SMP
area, and hope to have some patches against 2.1.119 in the next few days.

At one time (2.1.102 timeframe), we had SMP patches that seemed pretty
stable on RAWHIDE, SABLE, and DP264, but since then, we've been distracted
with other things, among them generic kernel.

> 2) Linux SMP distributes the interrupts to all processors, according
> to some postings I saw recently which were discussing changing that to
> send them all to one. However, Linux SMP is closer to SunOS4 in its
> multiprocessing capabilities than Solaris, for example.

My understanding is that the various hardware implementations of interrupt
delivery do allow dispatch to arbitrary CPU on INTEL, SPARC, and ALPHA.  But
as far as I know, at one time only SPARC code actually did something along
those lines, and not to spread the interrupt load over the CPUs, but to
deliver the interrupt on the CPU holding the kernel lock, which simplified
some situations a lot.

IIRC, the code I saw was SPARC only, and back in the 2.1.8x timeframe. Many
things have changed since then, and this may no longer be an accurate
statement, for SPARC or for INTEL. Please, if someone knows the real story,
let us know...

