> 

Humm. What is the default mode for g77/gcc for undeflow, 
terminate or zero? Thought Alphas cannot generate denormalized
outputs, at least with HW.

> > If a number cannot be represented normalized, you're in
> > trouble because IEEE FP format has an implied 1 bit in the
> > mantissa.
> 
> No, exponent == 0 and mantessa != 0 is a denormal, and does not
> have the implied 1 bit.

Yeah. That's an edge case. I usually call those things
integers!! Means you need to increase your bits.
If you're already at 64bit, you're in for major headaches.

While '-mieee' handles these things, it is much more
likely a code bug than a useful FP number. All the
complaining about povray was due to bad code, not really
a need for '-mieee' to get the 'right' answer. All
'-mieee' usually does is mask bugs from what I've
seen in my own code/other's code, and heard on this
list.

I would not recommend that flag in 99.99% of the
cases where FP exceptions are found in code. There
are always cases where it may be needed, but by far
it should not be used. Course I also care how fast
the code runs so practically nothing I do could
tolerate the trap stuff it inserts in the assembly
code. I also like getting the 'right' answer so
hiding un-initialized variables and screwed up
indexing is not helpful from my point of view...


Wes


