> It's actually quite easy to generate a denormalized number using exp().
> When the argument is barely more negative than required to exceed the
> representation limits in the result, the library function gives you a
> denormalized number.  Then your code has to be compiled with -mieee, or
> it traps when you try to use the result.
> 

Actually, wouldn't the exp() function have to be '-mieee' then,
not just your application? '-mieee' actually adds asm
instructions/directives to your code to stop pipelining to handle
exactly the instruction that runs into trouble so if
exp() returns them, it has to have those extra bits.

I'm not saying you can't think of a way to generate them
though. I'm saying most applications don't use numbers
in that range and if they do, you should increase your
FP word size, if possible. When your app generates them,
you should be looking at why it's doing that. The only
case I've seen that's useful is underflow in a recursive
algorithm and setting the result to zero is the 'right' 
thing to do for that sort of condition in the alogrithms 
I've worked with. 


Wes


